[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: learning.FILES_REFERENCE
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[FILES_REFERENCE]
overview: Where to put different types of information
purpose: Route discovered knowledge to the correct documentation file
principle: Each file has specific scope — don't mix concerns

[STANDARD_FILES]
# These 4 files exist in every project initialized from this framework.
# Domain-specific files (e.g. CRYPTO.toon, API.toon, DATABASE.toon) are added
# per project and documented in QUICK_LINKS.toon.

[FILE_CLAUDE_MD]
location: /CLAUDE.md (project root)
scope: Critical universal rules (apply to ALL work on project)
examples_update:
  - "Never modify migrations without explicit approval"
  - "All public APIs must have OpenAPI schema"
  - "Core module MUST NOT write to filesystem"
examples_not:
  - Module-specific rules (→ CONSTRAINTS.toon)
  - Algorithm details (→ domain-specific .toon)
  - Implementation patterns (→ ARCHITECTURE.toon)
when_update: Rare. Only if affects ALL code in project.
format: Markdown section under "Critical Rules"
size: Keep small (< 500 words total)

[FILE_CONSTRAINTS_TOON]
location: docs/agent/CONSTRAINTS.toon
scope: Architectural constraints and "never do this" rules
examples_update:
  - rule: "Module A never depends on Module B"
  - rule: "No allocations > 64 MB in single block"
  - rule: "Thread safety required for shared cache"
examples_not:
  - Implementation details (→ ARCHITECTURE.toon)
  - What modules do (→ ARCHITECTURE.toon)
when_update: When discovering a "you must not" or "only between X and Y" rule
format: [CONSTRAINT_N] section + rule + why + how_to_apply
size: One constraint = 5-10 lines max

[FILE_ARCHITECTURE_TOON]
location: docs/agent/ARCHITECTURE.toon
scope: Component/layer structure, dataflow, interactions
examples_update:
  - How data flows through modules/layers
  - What each layer owns vs observes
  - Caching patterns, ownership model
  - Concurrency model, state transitions
examples_not:
  - Constraints (→ CONSTRAINTS.toon)
  - Specific algorithms (→ domain file)
when_update: When understanding component interactions or data movement
format: Structured sections per component/layer
size: Medium (should fit in one document)

[FILE_PROJECT_TOON]
location: docs/agent/PROJECT.toon
scope: Project overview, modules, current status
examples_update:
  - New module added
  - Status milestone changed
  - Tech stack updated
when_update: When project scope or status changes
format: [PROJECT], [MODULES], [STATUS] sections
size: Small (overview, not detail)

[FILE_QUICK_LINKS_TOON]
location: docs/agent/QUICK_LINKS.toon
scope: Navigation — which file for which info, code location pointers
examples_update:
  - New domain-specific .toon file added
  - Component moved to new path
when_update: When a new .toon file is created or structure changes
format: [CODE], [DOCUMENTATION] sections with path mappings
size: Small (index only)

[FILE_PROJECT_STRUCTURE_MD]
location: /PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md (project root)
scope: File and directory tree with descriptions
examples_update:
  - New module directory created
  - File moved or renamed
  - Directory refactored
when_update: When project structure physically changes
format: Markdown with file tree and descriptions
size: Keep organized and up-to-date

[DOMAIN_SPECIFIC_FILES]
# Projects add domain files as needed. Common examples:
#   docs/agent/API.toon         — endpoint documentation
#   docs/agent/DATABASE.toon    — schema, models
#   docs/agent/CRYPTO.toon      — cryptographic details
#   docs/agent/MODULES.toon     — deep module details if project is complex
#   docs/agent/ACTIVE_EPICS.toon — current work tracking
# Register new files in QUICK_LINKS.toon so future sessions can find them.

[DECISION_MATRIX]
Information type → File

Critical universal rule (all code)
  → CLAUDE.md

"Never do X" / architectural limit
  → CONSTRAINTS.toon

How components interact / dataflow
  → ARCHITECTURE.toon

Project overview / module list / status
  → PROJECT.toon

File/directory structure changed
  → PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md

Navigation entry / new domain file
  → QUICK_LINKS.toon

Domain-specific knowledge (API, DB, crypto, etc.)
  → Domain .toon file (create if missing, register in QUICK_LINKS.toon)
